"{\"url\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/project/prj_1K09XZV1B1S023XT\",\"title\":\"Moser River — Isometric\",\"head\":[\"UNDER VALIDATION\",\"MARINE\",\"Moser River\",\"CarbonRun\",\"->\",\"PROJECT DETAILS\",\"Expected first certificates\",\"2026\",\"Project code\",\"7FPK\",\"PROTOCOL DETAILS\",\"Current protocol version\"],\"issued_header\":null,\"buffer_header\":null,\"crediting\":null,\"verifier\":null,\"code\":\"7FPK\",\"approval\":\"\",\"protocol_current\":\"River Alkalinity Enhancement v1.0\",\"protocol_baseline\":null,\"cert_issued\":null,\"cert_retired\":null,\"cert_buffer\":null,\"reversal\":null,\"showing\":[\"Showing 2 of 2\"],\"tables\":[{\"heads\":[\"Submission date\",\"Title\",\"Download\"],\"rows\":[[\"15 Sep 2025\",\"Public Comment Summary\",\"PDF | ->\"],[\"01 Aug 2025\",\"PDD\",\"PDF | ->\"]]}],\"links\":[{\"h\":\"https://carbonrun.io\",\"t\":\"CarbonRun\"},{\"h\":\"/protocol/river-alkalinity-enhancement/1.0\",\"t\":\"River Alkalinity Enhancement v1.0\"},{\"h\":\"/supplier/spl_1JFW0WS9H1S0GKKN\",\"t\":\"Learn more\"},{\"h\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/standard#validation-and-verification-process\",\"t\":\"Isometric Standard\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1JFW0WS9H1S0GKKN/prj_1K09XZV1B1S023XT/pjd_1K56BCTWJ1S0AJMS/public_comment_summary.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1JFW0WS9H1S0GKKN/prj_1K09XZV1B1S023XT/pjd_1K1KJ42BB1S00A1S/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"UNDER VALIDATION\\nMARINE\\nMoser River\\nCarbonRun\\n->\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nExpected first certificates\\n2026\\nProject code\\n7FPK\\nPROTOCOL DETAILS\\nCurrent protocol version\\nRiver Alkalinity Enhancement v1.0\\nSummary\\n\\nCarbonRun will implement River Alkalinity Enhancement (RAE) on the Moser River, Nova Scotia, starting Fall 2025. This involves adding finely crushed natural alkaline rock to the river, which reacts with CO₂ to form stable bicarbonate, increasing the river's dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC) transport capacity. Once transported to the ocean, this bicarbonate is stored for over 1,000 years. The project aims to permanently sequester carbon and improve water quality to support healthier ecosystems.\\n\\nNova Scotia, Canada\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nMarine carbon dioxide removal (mCDR) leverages the ocean's natural capacity to absorb and store carbon for thousands of years. These pathways use the biogeochemical and physical properties of the ocean and other bodies of water to capture carbon dioxide from the atmosphere or to permanently store carbon already contained within them. Approaches to mCDR include Ocean Alkalinity Enhancement and Direct Ocean Capture and Storage.\\n\\nLearn more\\nProject timeline\\nCompleted 30 Aug 2025\\nPublic comment period\\nCompleted 2025\\nInitial documentation submission\\nCompleted 2025\\nAudit kick-off\\nCompleted 2025\\nSite visit\\nExpected 2026\\nIN PROGRESS\\nApproval date\\n\\nIsometric officially approves the project having reviewed the validation report produced by the VVB, documenting the activities, results, and conclusion of the audit. A full list of content requirements for the report can be found in the Isometric Standard.\\n\\nExpected 2026\\nInitial credit issuance\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n15 Sep 2025\\tPublic Comment Summary\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n01 Aug 2025\\tPDD\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 2 of 2\\nPrevious\\nNext\"}"